        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> * 'folke/lazy.nvim' already handles `setup()` calls for 'mini.nvim' standalone repositories automatically. There are a couple of modules that require some explicit user configuration for them to be useful ('mini.base16', 'mini.hipatterns'), but the whole point is to have user decide what they want.\r\n>   So the only reason I see now for adding 'setup.lua.example' file(s) is to automatically create a file in which users can write their config.\r\n> * I'd rather adhere to something like [neovim/packspec](https://github.com/neovim/packspec) if/when it gets properly released.\r\n\r\nI agree in part that it may seem pretty pointless to create a setup file that just has something like: `return { \"some/plugin\" }` inside it. By default, if no `setup.lua.example` is detected, then `activate.nvim` creates a file with just that in.\r\n\r\nThe problem here is that the user then has no real idea whether additional configuration is required without going to read the docs. Because of this, I have left the contents of the return block commented by default and provided a link to the plugin docs to encourage the user to investigate further.\r\n\r\nI think it would be nicer if even if no additional setup is required, the plugin can indicate that in some machine readable way.\r\n\r\nI have been following packspec and when it gets released it'd be easy to support this, however, it doesn't seem to solve the default setup options problem which I think is one of the main barriers to entry for new and inexperienced (or just lazy!) users.\r\n\r\nDo you think it'd be worth proposing that packspec includes some kind of optional default setup options block? If it's outside the scope of packspec, do you think this is a problem that is even worth trying to solve?\r\n\r\nThanks for your reply, and all the great plugins!\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
