        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> The problem here is that the user then has no real idea whether additional configuration is required without going to read the docs.\r\n\r\nMy current opinion is that reading the docs is essential part of using Neovim and its ecosystem. Making it less painful for novice users and less needed for experienced users is a more preferable route for me.\r\n\r\n> I have been following packspec and when it gets released it'd be easy to support this, however, it doesn't seem to solve the default setup options problem which I think is one of the main barriers to entry for new and inexperienced (or just lazy!) users.\r\n> \r\n> Do you think it'd be worth proposing that packspec includes some kind of optional default setup options block? If it's outside the scope of packspec, do you think this is a problem that is even worth trying to solve?\r\n\r\nI would agree that having \"official\" unified requirements on how to structure plugin is beneficial in long-term. However, witnessing countless lines spent on \"autoload/plugin directory vs `setup()`\" discussions, this will probably lead nowhere.",
