        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "\r\n> My current opinion is that reading the docs is essential part of using Neovim and its ecosystem. Making it less painful for novice users and less needed for experienced users is a more preferable route for me.\r\n\r\nI agree, but I think there is a balance between user experience and documentation. I think starting with a working default config for users makes sense, also, in an example config inline documentation can give enough information to the user to configure the plugin, in many cases.\r\n\r\n> I would agree that having \"official\" unified requirements on how to structure plugin is beneficial in long-term. However, witnessing countless lines spent on \"autoload/plugin directory vs `setup()`\" discussions, this will probably lead nowhere.\r\n\r\nI think this is probably why it's a good idea to start with something imperfect, evolve it, and try different conventions in the wild to see what survives and what people like, rather than waiting for the mythical perfect solution that everyone can agree on!\r\n\r\nI really appreciate your insight and opinions!\r\n\r\n\r\n",
